# CTRL-A and CTRL-X in Normal mode (change.txt, "Adding and subtracting").

=== CTRL-A adds one to the number under the cursor
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-A*
quote:  Add [count] to the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  foo |7 bar
keys:   <C-a>
expect: foo |8 bar
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A on the middle of a number adds to the whole number
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-A*
quote:  Add [count] to the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  x 1|29 y
keys:   <C-a>
expect: x 13|0 y
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A uses the number after the cursor
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-A*
quote:  Add [count] to the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  |item 41 of 50
keys:   <C-a>
expect: item 4|2 of 50
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A with no number after the cursor does nothing
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-A*
quote:  Add [count] to the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  12 a|bc
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 12 a|bc
mode:   normal

=== a count adds that much
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-A*
quote:  Add [count] to the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  |7
keys:   5<C-a>
expect: 1|2
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-X subtracts one
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-X*
quote:  Subtract [count] from the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  |10
keys:   <C-x>
expect: |9
mode:   normal

=== a count subtracts that much
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-X*
quote:  Subtract [count] from the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  |100
keys:   25<C-x>
expect: 7|5
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-X below zero gives a negative number
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  The CTRL-A and CTRL-X commands work for (signed) decimal numbers
given:  |0
keys:   <C-x>
expect: -|1
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A on a negative number counts the sign
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  For decimals a leading negative sign is considered for incrementing or decrementing
given:  |-5
keys:   <C-a>
expect: -|4
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A takes a negative number through zero
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  For decimals a leading negative sign is considered for incrementing or decrementing
given:  |-1
keys:   2<C-a>
expect: |1
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A with the cursor on the minus sign
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  For decimals a leading negative sign is considered for incrementing or decrementing
given:  x |-10
keys:   <C-a>
expect: x -|9
mode:   normal

=== a minus after a letter is still a sign
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-A on "14" in "Carbon-14" results in "Carbon-15" (without "blank" it would become "Carbon-13").
given:  |Carbon-14
keys:   <C-a>
expect: Carbon-1|3
mode:   normal

=== a minus after a digit is a sign too
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-X on "2020" in "9-2020" results in "9-2019" (without "unsigned" it would become "9-2021").
given:  9-|2020
keys:   <C-x>
expect: 9-202|1
mode:   normal

=== x-1 counts the minus sign
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-A on "14" in "Carbon-14" results in "Carbon-15" (without "blank" it would become "Carbon-13").
given:  |x-1
keys:   <C-a>
expect: x|0
mode:   normal

=== with unsigned in 'nrformats' the minus is ignored
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  If included, numbers are recognized as unsigned. Thus a leading dash or negative sign won't be considered as part of the number.
given:  9-|2020
keys:   :set nf+=unsigned<CR><C-x>
expect: 9-201|9
mode:   normal

=== with blank in 'nrformats' a minus after a letter isn't a sign
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-A on "14" in "Carbon-14" results in "Carbon-15"
given:  |Carbon-14
keys:   :set nf+=blank<CR><C-a>
expect: Carbon-1|5
mode:   normal

=== letters aren't incremented by default
ref:    nvim/options.txt *'nrformats'*
quote:  alpha	If included, single alphabetical characters will be incremented or decremented.
given:  |abc
keys:   <C-a>
expect: |abc
mode:   normal

=== with alpha in 'nrformats' a letter is incremented
ref:    nvim/options.txt *'nrformats'*
quote:  single alphabetical characters will be incremented or decremented.
given:  |a)
keys:   :set nf+=alpha<CR><C-a>
expect: |b)
mode:   normal

=== with alpha in 'nrformats' z stays z
ref:    nvim/options.txt *'nrformats'*
quote:  alpha	If included, single alphabetical characters will be incremented or decremented.
given:  |z
keys:   :set nf+=alpha<CR><C-a>
expect: |z
mode:   normal

=== with alpha in 'nrformats' a number after the cursor still wins over a later letter
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  When 'nrformats' includes "alpha", Vim will change the alphabetic character under or after the cursor.
given:  |-x 5
keys:   :set nf+=alpha<CR><C-a>
expect: -|y 5
mode:   normal

=== hex numbers are incremented in hex
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  hex If included, numbers starting with "0x" or "0X" will be considered to be hexadecimal.
given:  |0x0f
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0x1|0
mode:   normal

=== hex keeps leading zeros
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Example: Using CTRL-X on "0x100" results in "0x0ff".
given:  |0x100
keys:   <C-x>
expect: 0x0f|f
mode:   normal

=== hex follows the case of the rightmost letter
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  The case of the rightmost letter in the number determines the case of the resulting hexadecimal number.
given:  |0xaF
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0xB|0
mode:   normal

=== 0X keeps its case
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  When 'nrformats' includes "hex", Vim assumes numbers starting with '0x' or '0X' are hexadecimal.
given:  |0Xff
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0X10|0
mode:   normal

=== hex with no letter is lower case by default
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  If there is no letter in the current number, Vim uses the previously detected case.
given:  |0x9
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0x|a
mode:   normal

=== hex ignores a minus sign
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  for binary, octal and hex values, it won't be considered.
given:  |-0x10
keys:   <C-a>
expect: -0x1|1
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A with the cursor on the x of a hex number
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  When 'nrformats' includes "hex", Vim assumes numbers starting with '0x' or '0X' are hexadecimal.
given:  0|x1f
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0x2|0
mode:   normal

=== without hex in 'nrformats' 0x10 is a decimal 0
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  hex If included, numbers starting with "0x" or "0X" will be considered to be hexadecimal.
given:  |0x10
keys:   :set nf-=hex<CR><C-a>
expect: |1x10
mode:   normal

=== binary numbers are incremented in binary
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  When 'nrformats' includes "bin", Vim assumes numbers starting with '0b' or '0B' are binary.
given:  |0b11
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0b10|0
mode:   normal

=== binary keeps leading zeros
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Example: Using CTRL-X on "0b1000" subtracts one, resulting in "0b0111".
given:  |0b1000
keys:   <C-x>
expect: 0b011|1
mode:   normal

=== 0B keeps its case
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  When 'nrformats' includes "bin", Vim assumes numbers starting with '0b' or '0B' are binary.
given:  |0B101
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0B11|0
mode:   normal

=== a hex number with b in it stays hex
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  CTRL-A on "0x0b11" results in "0x0b12", not "0x0b100".
given:  |0x0b11
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0x0b1|2
mode:   normal

=== leading zeros stay decimal without octal in 'nrformats'
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  octal If included, numbers that start with a zero will be considered to be octal.
given:  |007
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 00|8
mode:   normal

=== a decimal number keeps its width
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  For numbers with leading zeros (including all octal and hexadecimal numbers), Vim preserves the number of characters in the number when possible.
given:  |0099
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 010|0
mode:   normal

=== a decimal number keeps its width going down
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  Vim preserves the number of characters in the number when possible.
given:  |0100
keys:   <C-x>
expect: 009|9
mode:   normal

=== with octal in 'nrformats' 007 becomes 010
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Example: Using CTRL-A on "007" results in "010".
given:  |007
keys:   :set nf+=octal<CR><C-a>
expect: 01|0
mode:   normal

=== with octal in 'nrformats' 0077 becomes 0100
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  CTRL-A on "0077" results in "0100"
given:  |0077
keys:   :set nf+=octal<CR><C-a>
expect: 010|0
mode:   normal

=== with octal in 'nrformats' a number with an 8 is decimal and loses its zeros
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  leading zeros are removed to avoid that the result may be recognized as an octal number.
given:  |0089
keys:   :set nf+=octal<CR><C-a>
expect: 9|0
mode:   normal

=== a number too big for 64 bits is rounded down and not changed
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  E.g. CTRL-X on 18446744073709551616 results in 18446744073709551615.
given:  |18446744073709551616
keys:   <C-x>
expect: 1844674407370955161|5
mode:   normal

=== a much bigger number is rounded down the same way
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  Same for larger numbers, such as 18446744073709551618.
given:  |18446744073709551618
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 1844674407370955161|5
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A goes past the largest signed 64-bit number
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  The CTRL-A and CTRL-X commands work for (signed) decimal numbers
given:  |9223372036854775807
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 922337203685477580|8
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-X goes past the smallest signed 64-bit number
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  The CTRL-A and CTRL-X commands work for (signed) decimal numbers
given:  |-9223372036854775808
keys:   <C-x>
expect: -922337203685477580|9
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A on 2^64-1 wraps to the negative of it
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-X on "0" or CTRL-A on "18446744073709551615" (2^64 - 1) has no effect, overflow is prevented.
given:  |18446744073709551615
keys:   <C-a>
expect: -1844674407370955161|5
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-X on minus 2^64-1 wraps to 2^64-1
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  The CTRL-A and CTRL-X commands work for (signed) decimal numbers
given:  |-18446744073709551615
keys:   <C-x>
expect: 1844674407370955161|5
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A on the largest 64-bit hex number wraps to zero
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  Vim preserves the number of characters in the number when possible.
given:  |0xffffffffffffffff
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0x000000000000000|0
mode:   normal

=== a hex number too big for 64 bits is rounded down and not changed
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  When the number under the cursor is too big to fit into 64 bits, it will be rounded off to the nearest number that can be represented, and the addition/subtraction is skipped.
given:  |0x10000000000000000
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 0x0fffffffffffffff|f
mode:   normal

=== with unsigned the largest number doesn't wrap
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-X on "0" or CTRL-A on "18446744073709551615" (2^64 - 1) has no effect, overflow is prevented.
given:  |18446744073709551615
keys:   :set nf+=unsigned<CR><C-a>
expect: 1844674407370955161|5
mode:   normal

=== with unsigned 0 doesn't go below zero
ref:    nvim/options.txt *octal-nrformats*
quote:  Using CTRL-X on "0" or CTRL-A on "18446744073709551615" (2^64 - 1) has no effect, overflow is prevented.
given:  |0
keys:   :set nf+=unsigned<CR><C-x>
expect: |0
mode:   normal

=== . repeats CTRL-A with its count
ref:    repeat.txt *.*
quote:  Without a count, the count of the last change is used.
given:  |10
keys:   5<C-a>
expect: 1|5
dot:    2|0
mode:   normal

=== . with a count replaces the CTRL-A count
ref:    repeat.txt *.*
quote:  If you enter a count, it will replace the last one.
given:  |10
keys:   5<C-a>2.
expect: 1|7
mode:   normal

=== . repeats CTRL-X on another number
ref:    repeat.txt *.*
quote:  Simple changes can be repeated with the "." command.
given:  |5 and 9
keys:   <C-x>w.
expect: 4 and |8
mode:   normal

=== u undoes CTRL-A
ref:    nvim/change.txt *CTRL-A*
quote:  Add [count] to the number or alphabetic character at or after the cursor.
given:  |99
keys:   <C-a>
expect: 10|0
undo:   |99
mode:   normal

=== CTRL-A in a macro numbers a list
ref:    nvim/change.txt *v_g_CTRL-X*
quote:  The CTRL-A command is very useful in a macro.
given:  |1. item
keys:   qaYp<C-a>q3@a
expect:
  1. item
  2. item
  3. item
  4. item
  |5. item
mode:   normal
